.TH NAS-SMARTMOUNT 8 "2026-11-13" "smart-mount 1.15" "System Administration Manual"
.SH NAME
nas-smartmount \- mount NFS and CIFS shares only on the right network, from the
right server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nas-smartmount.sh
.RB show " ["
.B \-\-json
.RB "]"
.br
.B nas-smartmount.sh
.RB { mount | unmount | apply | setup | \-\-version | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nas-smartmount.sh
mounts each configured share only when the machine is on a network where the server is
reachable
.I and
identified, and unmounts it otherwise.
Any number of NFS and CIFS (SMB) shares, each with its own mount point.
Nothing is read from
.I /etc/fstab
at run time: the tool mounts each share itself.
.PP
Identity is verified at layer 2, not by name or address alone: the server's MAC address
is read from the ARP table of the link in use, and compared with the MAC expected for
that share.
A share without a target MAC is never mounted.
.PP
All the policy lives in one place.
.B apply
decides for every share, and is the only verb that mounts or unmounts on its own
initiative.
The two NetworkManager hooks and the systemd timer only call it: that is what keeps them
from undoing each other's work when both links are up (docked laptop, Wi-Fi on another
network).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B show
Print, for every configured share, its mount point, source, state
.RI ( "mounted, healthy" ,
.I "mounted, NOT RESPONDING"
when the mount no longer answers, or
.IR "not mounted" )
and the options retained in the configuration.
Any NFS or CIFS mount that does not come from the configuration is listed as
.IR "mounted (not in configuration)" .
The current Wi-Fi state is printed above the table: interface and SSID. Nothing more —
what a share expects is its own business, and the
.B AUTHORIZED NETWORKS
column says it.
That line is the machine's state; the
.B AUTHORIZED NETWORKS
column carries the networks accepted by each share
.RI ( "(global)" ,
or a list, or
.RI * ),
which is what actually decides whether that share may mount here — so a share that did not
mount is explained without reading the journal.
Read-only: nothing is mounted, unmounted or logged.
It also works without root: the configuration is then unreadable, only the real mounts
are listed, and a note says so.
.TP
.BI show " \-\-json"
The same information as JSON, for a script or a graphical front end.
Each share carries
.IR type ,
.IR server ,
.IR share ,
.IR mountpoint ,
.IR networks ,
.IR source ,
.IR fstype ,
.I options
(as retained),
.I runtime_options
(as reported by the kernel),
.IR state ,
.I mounted
and
.IR healthy .
The credentials path never appears.
These values are never translated, and the current Wi-Fi state is reported in a
.B wifi
object
.RI ( iface ", " ssid ", " state ", " expected ),
and each share carries
.I networks
(as the effective list, the global default being written
.IR "(global)" ),
.I network_allowed
(whether the current SSID is accepted for it, or
.B null
when the SSID cannot be read).
.TP
.B apply
Evaluate the policy for every share and mount, repair or unmount as required.
This is what the timer and the hooks call.
.TP
.B mount
Mount the shares that are legitimately reachable; never unmount anything.
.TP
.B unmount
Unmount every configured mount point.
.TP
.B setup
The configuration wizard (whiptail). It starts from the configuration in place, so each
question is a confirmation; it writes
.I /etc/smart-mount/config
(mode 0640, the previous file kept as
.IR .bak )
and neutralises
.I /etc/fstab
entries that would double-mount a share.
Needs root.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version number on one line
.RI ( "smart-mount " version ),
without reading the configuration: the line to paste into a bug report.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the short help, the configuration format and the exit status.
.SH LANGUAGE
Messages, errors and the
.B show
output follow the locale
.RB ( LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_MESSAGES ,
.BR LANG :
two letters are enough), English by default, so that a journal pasted into a bug report
stays readable.
.B SMARTMOUNT_LANG
forces the other language.
This page exists in both languages: the installed layout lets
.BR man (1)
pick the one matching your locale.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.I /etc/smart-mount/config
is a shell file, read as root, mode 0640.
One record per share, seven or eight fields separated by
.BR | :
.PP
.RS
type|server|share|mount point|options|mac|credentials|networks
.RE
.PP
.TP
.I type
.B nfs
or
.BR cifs .
.TP
.I server
IP address or host name of the server.
.TP
.I share
Exported path for NFS, share name for CIFS.
.TP
.I options
Passed as
.BI \-o " options"
to
.BR mount (8).
Shown by
.BR show .
On NFS as on CIFS,
.BR nodev ,
.B nosuid
and
.B noexec
are enforced by the kernel.
.B noatime
and
.B lazytime
are ignored by mount.cifs: the mount stays in
.BR relatime .
CIFS has its own options:
.BR vers= ,
.BR iocharset= ,
.BR uid= ,
.BR gid= ,
.BR file_mode= ,
.BR dir_mode= .
.TP
.I mac
MAC address of the server.
Mandatory: a share without one is refused with exit status 4.
.TP
.I credentials
CIFS only: path of a credentials file, passed as
.IR credentials= .
Never as
.IR \-o " username=...,password=..." ,
because a command line is readable by every user through
.BR ps (1).
.TP
.I networks
Optional: networks where this share may mount, separated by
.B ;
or
.BR , .
Empty means the global
.BR WIFI_NAME ;
a single
.B *
means any network, the server's identity deciding alone.
An SSID must contain neither
.B ;
nor
.BR , .
.PP
Global variables:
.BR WIFI_NAME ,
.BR WIFI_IFACE ,
.B LAN_IFACE .
.PP
A configuration file from an earlier version (flat
.BR NFS_SERVER_IP ,
.BR MOUNT_POINT ", ...)"
is still read as a single NFS share.
.SH NETWORK IDENTITY
Wi-Fi is accepted when the SSID is allowed for the share
.I and
the server's MAC is confirmed on
.BR WIFI_IFACE .
Ethernet is accepted when the server's MAC is confirmed on
.BR LAN_IFACE .
If either link matches, the share is mounted; if both are ruled out without ambiguity, it
is unmounted; if a link cannot be read, nothing is done: a failed read must never destroy
a working mount.
.PP
Anything that changes the MAC seen by this host invalidates the check: a server behind a
router or a VLAN, a VPN, NAT.
Configure the MAC actually visible on the link.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/smart-mount/config
Share definitions, read as root.
.TP
.I /etc/smart-mount/credentials-*
CIFS credentials, mode 0600.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/nas-smartmount.sh
The program.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/nfs-smartmount.sh
Compatibility symlink (the command was renamed when CIFS support arrived).
.TP
.I /usr/bin/smart-mount-setup
Wrapper around
.BR "nas-smartmount.sh setup" .
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/98-eth-nfs-mac
Ethernet hook.
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/99-wifi-nfs-mount
Wi-Fi hook.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/nfs-network-check.{service,timer}
Periodic policy run, every 15 minutes.
.TP
.I /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.jotenakis.smartmount.policy
Polkit action allowing
.B pkexec nas-smartmount.sh
in a local session.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_DRY_RUN
When
.BR 1 ,
evaluate and log the decision for every share without mounting or unmounting.
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_CONFIG
Alternative path for the configuration file; intended for tests.
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_DEPS
List of expected runtime dependencies, replacing the usual check.
Intended for tests.
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_GROUP
Group owning the configuration file, used by the wizard.
Defaults to
.BR root .
.TP
.B SMARTMOUNT_LANG
Force the message language
.RB ( fr
or
.IR en )
instead of deriving it from the locale.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a deliberate decision not to mount.
.TP
.B 1
A share could not be mounted.
.TP
.B 2
A mount point could not be unmounted.
.TP
.B 3
Invalid usage, or an invalid share record.
.TP
.B 4
A share has no target MAC.
.TP
.B 5
A runtime dependency is missing; the message names it.
.TP
.B 122
Configuration missing, or no share configured.
.TP
.B 127
The server was unreachable before mounting.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show what is mounted, with the options retained for each share:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ sudo nas-smartmount.sh show
TYPE  MOUNT POINT     SOURCE                             STATE             OPTIONS
nfs   /media/NAS      192.168.50.85:/media/diskusb/data  mounted, healthy  nodev,nosuid,noexec
cifs  /media/NAS-smb  //192.168.50.85/usbdisk_data       mounted, healthy  guest,uid=1000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check the policy without touching anything:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ sudo SMARTMOUNT_DRY_RUN=1 nas-smartmount.sh apply
.EE
.RE
.PP
Feed a graphical front end:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ nas-smartmount.sh show --json | jq '.shares[] | {mountpoint, state}'
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
The MAC check assumes the server is on the same layer 2 segment.
A cold ARP cache needs a ping to fill; a server that filters ICMP and is not already in
the ARP table cannot be confirmed, so its share does not mount.
An NFS share mounted
.B hard
can freeze a process for as long as the RPC timeouts last; the hooks unmount on network
loss, the timer repairs dead mounts, and
.B umount \-f
(abandon pending RPCs) is only attempted on NFS.
Interface names are global, but the list of accepted networks is per share: that is what
lets one share mount at home and elsewhere.
Two records must never share a mount point — the one that does not recognise the network
would unmount the other share.
A share holds one server address: a NAS that gets a different IP per network needs a
reserved DHCP lease, not a second record.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (8),
.BR umount (8),
.BR findmnt (8),
.BR nfs (5),
.BR mount.cifs (8),
.BR NetworkManager (8),
.BR systemd.timer (5)
